    "description": "The Catalog Console manages product categories, each of which is one row with a code, display name, active flag, and sort order. Editors create, update, deactivate, and list categories, and most rules concern only that row. The team wants product domain behavior, validation, and persistence access to remain in one compact component because there is no meaningful object graph or multi-row invariant. Database transactions may still be controlled by the application service, but the chosen persistence arrangement should keep SQL and table details out of HTTP handlers and presentation code. A recent incident saw a deactivation endpoint bypass the shared sort-order check with its own inline SQL, leaving gaps in the public listing until editors re-saved each row by hand.",
    "decision_statement": "Choose where row state, row-local behavior, and CRUD operations belong for a simple catalog category.",
    "notes": "active-record still fits the one-row category keeping state, validation, and CRUD together, with data-mapper the translation-layer alternative; fixed by expanding the description past the 600-char minimum with a validation-bypass incident."
